
- bitemporal databases

:: core concepts, time axes ::

bitemporality (means) tracking two independent time axes per fact
valid time (means) when a fact was true in the modelled world
system time (aka) transaction time, record time
system time (means) when the DB knew/recorded the fact
valid time (contrast) system time
unitemporal (means) only one time axis tracked, typically system time
bitemporal (pt-of) temporal database family
"     (role) separate "what was true" from "what we knew and when"

as-of queries (means) query state of DB as it was known at time T
valid-time queries (means) query what was true in the world at time T
bitemporal query (combines) as-of + valid-time to reconstruct both axes
corrections (example) backdating, retroactive amendments, forward-dated facts
immutability (enables) audit trails, reproducibility, time-travel debugging

:: foundational work, research ::

Richard Snodgrass (role) authored "Developing Time-Oriented Database Applications in SQL", 1999
    "         (role) long-time proponent of temporal SQL standards
Jim Gray, Surajit Chaudhuri (note) early MSR work on temporal extensions
TSQL2 (means) a 1990s proposal to extend SQL with temporal primitives
TSQL2 (led to) SQL:2011 temporal features
SQL:2011 (adds) system-versioned tables, application-time period tables
SQL:2011 (limitation) leaves bitemporal semantics partly to vendors

// Key insight: bitemporality lets you answer "what did we know, when did we know it"
// which is the difference between an operational correction and a rewrite of history.

:: industry implementations ::

XTDB (role) immutable bitemporal document database from JUXT
  "  (lang) Clojure, Datalog, SQL
  "  (feature) valid + system time as first-class
  "  (feature) SQL:2011 bitemporal semantics
XTDB 2 (note) rewritten on Apache Arrow columnar storage
  "   (feature) HTAP-style architecture, separation of storage and compute

MarkLogic (feature) bitemporal document support since 8.0
Snowflake (feature) unitemporal time travel via system time only
Datomic (note) asserts/retracts with transaction time; not strictly bitemporal
  "     (contrast) XTDB which adds valid-time
MariaDB, DB2, SQL Server (feature) system-versioned temporal tables as a SQL:2011 subset
Postgres (note) no native bitemporal support; extensions like temporal_tables exist

:: use cases, regulatory drivers ::

financial services (driver) MiFID II, BCBS 239 reporting require as-of reconstruction
insurance (driver) claims with effective dates differing from record dates
healthcare (driver) clinical records where observation time ≠ entry time
compliance (driver) GDPR right-to-erasure complicates immutable stores
decision audit (driver) "why did the system do X at time T given what it knew"

:: trends, tensions ::

trend (item) convergence of event sourcing + bitemporality
trend (item) immutable crypto-influenced ledgers meeting temporal SQL
trend (item) push toward open table formats Iceberg/Delta with time travel
tension (item) time travel ≠ bitemporal — most lakehouses only track system time
tension (item) GDPR erasure vs. immutable audit requirements
research (item) temporal joins, Allen's interval algebra in SQL optimisers
research (item) indexing strategies for bitemporal range queries such as MVBT, BT-tree

